\section*{Exact satisfiability threshold for random k-SAT for small k (k = 3)}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: Each fixed random clause size $k\geq3$ is claimed to have a positive finite limiting satisfiability threshold; the manuscript credits an earlier resolution by Carenini; The limiting random 3-SAT threshold is claimed computable to any requested accuracy by one finite deterministic procedure. Together they provide the following support within the scopes described below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Fix integer \(k\ge3\). Sample independent proper \(k\)-clauses with replacement, choosing \(k\) distinct variables uniformly and each literal sign uniformly. If \(P_n(m)\) denotes the satisfiability probability, the source claims a finite positive constant \(\alpha_k\) with
\[
 \lim_{n\to\infty}P_n(\lfloor cn\rfloor)=
 \begin{cases}1,&0\le c<\alpha_k,\\0,&c>\alpha_k.\end{cases}
\]
No value at \(c=\alpha_k\), or numerical formula for \(\alpha_k\), is part of this theorem.

\paragraph{Result 2.} For uniform proper signed random 3-SAT clauses sampled independently with replacement, write \(p(n,m)\) for satisfiability probability. The source asserts \(\alpha_3\in(0,\infty)\) with
\[
 p(n,\lfloor an\rfloor)\to1\ (a<\alpha_3),\qquad
 p(n,\lfloor an\rfloor)\to0\ (a>\alpha_3).
\]
It also claims a single deterministic Turing machine that, on unary input \(1^r\), halts with rational \(q_r\) satisfying
\[
 |q_r-\alpha_3|\le2^{-r}\qquad(r\ge1).
\]
This machine has no oracle, advice, or noncomputable constant as input; no polynomial running-time bound is asserted. The clause-density parameter is restricted to $a\ge0$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Replacement of shorter clauses and integrated forcing control a capped last-satisfiable index. Clause-omission pivotal estimates give concentration, while comparisons between system sizes make the normalized centers converge, producing an alternative existence proof.

Deletion bounds certify rational lower brackets, and finite hierarchical soft-pressure trials certify upper brackets. A fair enumeration of both certificate families halts at every accuracy, using existence of the limiting threshold without needing an effective finite-size convergence rate.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The cited theorem establishes existence of a sharp limiting threshold for each k>=3 in precisely the independent proper-clause model, but it does not identify the exact requested 3-SAT threshold value. The cited theorem establishes a computable limiting threshold via an oracle-free precision machine; the introduction explicitly says the constant is not evaluated or identified with the predicted value, whereas our target asks for the exact clause-to-variable ratio. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 235, the selected entries establish threshold existence, threshold computability, linear variance for $k\geq4$, and the earlier $O(n\log n)$ bound for $k=3$. The later linear three-clause variance improvement is not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{FixedClauseThreshold.lean}, \texttt{SATComputability.lean}, \texttt{SATSharpness.lean}, \texttt{SATVariance.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

